  "id": "COMPOUND:22559 - PROTEIN:1234",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which 6,6'-Methoxygossypol may interact with Potassium channel subfamily K member 3, through a series of target interactions involving sodium channel subunit beta-2. This pathway is supported by multiple high-scoring connections, with 6,6'-Methoxygossypol acting as a target for sodium channel subunit beta-2, which in turn is a target of several compounds, including (Z)-N-[(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-9-methyldec-7-enamide and Longispinogenin. All these compounds are implicated in the regulation of potassium channels, suggesting a common mechanism of action involving the modulation of sodium channels, which are known to influence the activity of potassium channels. The presence of multiple distinct compounds targeting the same downstream target implies that 6,6'-Methoxygossypol may exert its effects through a similar mechanism, possibly by modulating sodium channel activity, which in turn affects potassium channel function. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that 6,6'-Methoxygossypol may act as a modulator of potassium channels through a sodium channel-dependent pathway.",
